1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,319
Many metals react with water to form a metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas.

2
00:00:04,319 --> 00:00:09,762
Potassium reacts even more violently than sodium: it whizzes around on the water's surface,

3
00:00:09,762 --> 00:00:12,128
and the heat can make the hydrogen burn.

4
00:00:13,187 --> 00:00:14,726
Calcium is less reactive.

5
00:00:14,726 --> 00:00:21,115
It reacts steadily with water, forming hydrogen bubbles and a milky white precipitate of calcium hydroxide.

6
00:00:21,115 --> 00:00:25,730
Test the solutions with Universal Indicator, and you'll find they're alkaline.

7
00:00:26,789 --> 00:00:32,054
Magnesium reacts only slowly with cold water. Heated, it reacts with hot water or steam,

8
00:00:32,054 --> 00:00:37,497
but forms magnesium oxide instead of the hydroxide, an unexpected result, called an anomaly.

9
00:00:38,556 --> 00:00:44,413
Zinc and iron only react with hot water, and copper, silver and gold don't react with water at all.

10
00:00:45,473 --> 00:00:48,608
A small piece of calcium is dropped into cold water,

11
00:00:48,608 --> 00:00:52,690
and the hydrogen it gives off is collected and measured every minute.

12
00:00:53,749 --> 00:01:00,198
The volume rises quickly at first, then levels off at about 80 cubic centimetres when the calcium is used up.

13
00:01:01,257 --> 00:01:04,570
When the same mass of magnesium ribbon is used instead,

14
00:01:04,570 --> 00:01:08,712
only a few bubbles form: less than 1 cubic centimetre in five minutes.

15
00:01:09,771 --> 00:01:13,261
Pause and decide: which metal is more reactive with water,

16
00:01:13,261 --> 00:01:16,397
and what evidence from the data supports your answer?
